Precession no longer includes the frame bias between the ICRS and the mean equator and equinox of J2000.0. This changes the results of conversions between FK5 coordinates of different equinoxes, and between FK5 and ecliptic coordinates, by up to 23 milli-arcseconds. The PREC conversion of a SlaMap applied the bias whenever one of its two epochs was exactly 2000.0, as it is in the conversions that a SkyFrame makes, so that precession from 2000.0 to 2000.0 was not a unit transformation. For two other epochs it multiplied the two matrices in the wrong order, with errors of up to 6 milli-arcseconds between 1950 and 2050 and 47 milli-arcseconds between 1900 and 2100. The ECLEQ and EQECL conversions precess from J2000.0 to the date, and were affected in the same way. The results now agree with the precession matrices of ERFA to rounding. The fault is in the palPrec function of the PAL library, a corrected copy of which is included in AST.
